.TH NLS 1 2026-11-25 "nls 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nls \- turn plain-English requests into exact shell commands, offline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nls
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ tool ]
.RI \(dq request \(dq
.br
.B nls
.BR \-l | \-\-list
.RI [ tool ]
.br
.B nls \-\-shell
.BR zsh | bash
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nls
compiles a request such as
.RI \(dq "cut input.mp4 from 00:10 to 00:25" \(dq
into the command that does it, and prints it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ nls ffmpeg "cut input.mp4 from 00:10 to 00:25"
ffmpeg \-ss 00:10 \-to 00:25 \-i input.mp4 \-c copy input_cut.mp4
.EE
.RE
.PP
It works fully offline, without a language model: every command template
is compiled into the binary, and the request is matched against them by
keyword, with typed values (files, times, sizes, URLs, JSON paths, ...)
filled in and shell-quoted.
.PP
.B nls
never runs anything unless asked to with
.BR \-x .
.SH TOOLS
.TP
.B ffmpeg
cut, convert, compress, scale and speed up video and audio, make GIFs
(also reachable as
.BR ffprobe ).
.TP
.B jq
pick fields, filter with conditions, count, sort, group, convert to CSV.
.TP
.B grep
search inside files, with modifiers such as
.I "ignoring case"
or
.IR "whole word" .
.TP
.B find
find (and delete) files by name, type, size, age or line count.
.TP
.B tar
create, list and extract archives.
.TP
.B git
undo, squash, stash, branch, log, diff, blame, restore, push.
.TP
.B curl
GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, downloads, uploads, status checks.
.TP
.B magick
ImageMagick: resize, convert, crop, rotate, compress, favicons, animations
(also reachable as
.BR convert ).
.PP
Run
.B nls \-\-list
.I tool
to see every template with an example request.
.SH INFERRING THE TOOL
The tool may be left out:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ nls "convert demo.mov to gif"
ffmpeg \-i demo.mov \-vf '...' \-loop 0 demo.gif
.EE
.RE
.PP
The request is then compiled for every tool, and the one that fits best is
used: its best template's keyword score, plus what the request names \(em a
media file, an image, a JSON file or path, an archive, a URL, git words
such as
.I commit
or
.IR branch ,
a tool's own name
.RI ( "use jq to ..." ).
jq, ffmpeg, magick, git and curl only count when the request names
something of theirs.
.PP
When another tool fits about as well and gives a different command, its
command is shown on stderr, and
.B \-y
no longer skips the confirmation. When nothing points at a tool,
.B nls
refuses rather than guess; name the tool then.
.PP
The first word of a one-argument request can also be the tool
.RI ( "nls \(dqgit undo last commit\(dq" ),
except
.I find
and
.IR convert ,
which are read as verbs there
.RI ( "find TODO in src" " is a grep)."
A first word naming a tool
.B nls
doesn't support
.RI ( rsync ,
.IR openssl ,
.IR docker ,
\&...) is refused.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-exec
Run the command: show it on stderr, ask for confirmation, then run it with
the shell. Enter runs it.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
With
.BR \-x :
don't ask. It is ignored for an uncertain match (see
.BR \-v )
and for a destructive command.
.TP
.B \-\-force
With
.B \-x \-y:
don't ask for destructive commands either (and skip their dry run). It
never skips the question for an uncertain match.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-explain
After the command, explain it part by part: each option with its value,
jq programs stage by stage, ffmpeg filters, ImageMagick geometries, find
tests and git revisions. With
.BR \-x ,
the explanation goes to stderr with the command.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
On stderr: the extracted slots and words, every template's score, the
dry run of a destructive command, and without a tool, every tool's score.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list " [\fItool\fR]"
List the templates (of one tool) with an example request each;
destructive ones are marked.
.TP
.BI \-\-shell " shell"
Print the shell integration for
.B zsh
or
.B bash
(see
.BR "SHELL INTEGRATION" ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the usage.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options: what follows is the request even if it starts with
.BR \- .
.SH SHELL INTEGRATION
.RS
.EX
eval "$(nls \-\-shell zsh)"     # in ~/.zshrc
eval "$(nls \-\-shell bash)"    # in ~/.bashrc (bash 4.0 or later)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Type a request on the command line and press
.BR Ctrl+G :
the line is replaced by the command, to edit or run with Enter. A leading
.I nls
on the line is ignored, so
.I "nls git undo last commit"
works as well. Messages from
.B nls
(an uncertain match, what is missing) are shown under the line in zsh and
above it in bash; when no command can be made, the line stays as typed. In
zsh, undo
.RB ( Ctrl+_ )
brings the request back.
.SH DESTRUCTIVE COMMANDS
Commands that delete or overwrite data, or rewrite a remote (find deletes,
.BR "git reset \-\-hard" ,
.BR "git restore" ,
.BR "git clean" ,
.BR "git branch \-D" ,
.BR "git push \-\-delete" ,
force pushes,
.BR "curl \-X DELETE" )
are marked by
.BR \-\-list .
With
.BR \-x ,
.B nls
says what the command destroys, runs a dry run first where the tool has
one (its output goes to stderr; if it fails, the command isn't run), and
runs the command only on a typed
.BR y .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NLS_KEY
The key the shell integration binds, read when its script is evaluated:
zsh
.B bindkey
syntax
.RI ( "\(aq^X^N\(aq" ),
or readline syntax for bash
.RI ( "\(aq\eC\-x\eC\-n\(aq" ).
The default is Ctrl+G.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command was printed (or, with
.BR \-x ,
run and succeeded).
.TP
.B 1
No command: the request wasn't understood, lacked something (said on
stderr), or was refused as unsafe or ambiguous; or a dry run failed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown option or tool, or a request that is too long.
.TP
.B 130
With
.BR \-x :
the confirmation was declined.
.PP
Otherwise, with
.BR \-x ,
the command's own exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
nls jq "get .name from users.json where .age > 30"
nls grep "which python files contain TODO, ignoring case"
nls find "files larger than 100MB modified in the last 7 days"
nls tar "compress src/ into backup.tar.gz excluding .git"
nls git "undo last 2 commits keep changes staged"
nls curl "post data.json to https://api.example.com/users"
nls magick "resize hero.png to width 800 convert to webp quality 85"
nls "extract backup.tar.gz into restore/"
nls \-\-explain git "squash the last 3 commits"
nls \-x jq "pretty print data.json"
curl \-s https://api.example.com/users | eval "$(nls jq 'list .email of each user')"
.EE
.PP
Quote the whole request so the shell doesn't interpret
.BR > ,
.BR \(aq ,
.B *
or
.BR $ .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR jq (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR tar (1),
.BR git (1),
.BR curl (1),
.BR magick (1)
.PP
https://github.com/gnatal/nls
